WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.393
Local language-model performance combines responsiveness, throughput, quality, stability,

00:00:03.393 --> 00:00:04.216
and usability.

00:00:04.216 --> 00:00:08.743
Measure time to first token, prompt processing, generation speed, end-to-end completion,

00:00:08.743 --> 00:00:12.859
memory use, thermals, and errors on a repeatable real workload.

00:00:12.859 --> 00:00:18.210
A single peak number cannot describe whether the system serves its user well.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.857
Find the actual bottleneck.

00:00:19.857 --> 00:00:24.384
VRAM, GPU bandwidth, CPU, system RAM, storage, PCI Express transfers, cooling,

00:00:24.384 --> 00:00:26.854
and power can constrain different phases.

00:00:26.854 --> 00:00:31.793
Choose a model and quantization that leave headroom for context and cache.

00:00:31.793 --> 00:00:36.732
A smaller fully accelerated model often outperforms a larger model split across

00:00:36.732 --> 00:00:39.613
slow resources while remaining easier to operate.

00:00:39.613 --> 00:00:41.671
Manage context as a resource.

00:00:41.671 --> 00:00:46.199
Send relevant, well-scoped prompts and retrieval results instead of maximizing length.

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:50.314
Tune offload, batch, threads, cache, and backend against the baseline,

00:00:50.314 --> 00:00:52.372
one variable at a time.

00:00:52.372 --> 00:00:57.723
Select a runtime because it supports the hardware, model format, and workflow reliably,

00:00:57.723 --> 00:01:00.604
not because it exposes the most complexity.

00:01:00.604 --> 00:01:03.897
Validate changes through repeated tests and sustained runs.

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:08.425
Account for warm caches, background processes, power modes, and thermal throttling.

00:01:08.425 --> 00:01:11.717
Track known-good drivers and runtimes and preserve rollback.

00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:15.833
The best configuration is not necessarily the fastest isolated benchmark.

00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:21.184
It delivers acceptable quality at usable speed, behaves predictably through the real workload,

00:01:21.184 --> 00:01:25.300
and remains understandable and maintainable when software or requirements change.
